{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788295687,"nonce":"lgAs1OjvfspFeAwdxnK889Eh+oE=","message":"ALL THREE PARTS SHIPPED EXCEPT PART 3, NONE PROVEN ON A BENCH. Session 20, commits `52606d1` (source) and `db08b3d` (assembly). No verb here has been run against a live game and no acceptance suite exists yet.\n\n## The floors come from the GAME, with exactly one deviation\n\n`Verse/WorkTypeDef.requireCapableColonist` is RimWorld's own \"somebody must be able to do this\", enforced by `Verse/StartingPawnUtility.WorkTypeRequirementsSatisfied`. Twelve vanilla types carry it — Firefighter, Warden, Construction, Growing, Mining, PlantCutting, Crafting, Hauling, Cleaning, plus Childcare from Biotech — floor 1, on CAPABILITY, because capability is what the game checks. A modded type that sets the flag is picked up free. Note what that check is NOT: it runs ONCE, at world-gen, against the STARTING pawns, and never again.\n\n**`Doctor.requireCapableColonist` is FALSE.** The game does not require a doctor to start a colony at all. Doctor at 2 is the one deviation and it is not a house rule either: `RimWorld/Alert_NeedDoctor.Patients` tests `(item.Spawned || item.BrieflyDespawned()) \u0026\u0026 !item.Downed \u0026\u0026 item.workSettings != null \u0026\u0026 item.workSettings.WorkIsActive(Doctor)`, and **`!item.Downed` is in the game's own predicate**. One doctor's coverage is zero the moment that doctor is the patient — the M1 death exactly. Doctor's floor is therefore on AVAILABILITY.\n\n\"Enabled but incapable\" is concrete, not hypothetical: **every vanilla Doctor work-giver except `VisitSickPawn` requires `Manipulation`** — `DoctorTendEmergency`, `DoctorTendToHumanlikes`, `DoctorRescue`, `DoBillsMedicalHumanOperation` and eight more. A doctor with no hands has the work type ON, undisabled, and cannot tend. Reproduced from `RimWorld/WorkGiver.MissingRequiredCapacity` over the union of the type's work-givers, read through `PawnCapacityUtility.CalculateCapacityLevel` rather than `capacities.CapableOf`, because `Verse/PawnCapacitiesHandler.GetLevel` lazily builds `cachedCapacityLevels` and observers do not write.\n\n## Part 1 — `digest.work_coverage`\n\n`ok`, `under` (names only, so a caller can branch without walking rows), and `rows`. A row that is FINE costs three fields; a row that is UNDER carries the whole diagnosis — `floor`, `floor_on` (capable|available), `floor_by`, `have`, `short_by`, `capable`, `enabled`, `available`, `available_pawns`, `candidates` (ranked, with skill/passion) and `enabled_but_incapable` naming the missing capacity. **The cap only ever drops rows that are fine**; an under-covered row is never truncated. Also registered as a predicate section, so `advance {until:{condition:{path:\"work_coverage.ok\", op:\"==\", value:false}}}` works — it is cheap on the axis that matters (no `Room.Role`, no `GetStatValueAbstract`).\n\n## Part 2 — `work-cover`, and it is a VERB not a hook\n\nThe issue reads as though a Harmony hook should repair coverage on a roster change. It is deliberately not, per session 13's ruling on `threat-pardon`: **the decision must be a recorded ACT, not a silent exemption.** A mod that quietly reassigns work produces a colony no transcript explains, and the next post-mortem cannot tell the agent's decision from the mod's. What makes it unforgettable instead is that `work_coverage` is in EVERY digest and that `advance` halts on an own-faction downing (`722c951`, not yet built) — the trigger is the agent, and the agent is made to look.\n\n`work-cover {work?} {priority?} {dry_run?}` promotes the best remaining capable, available, capacity-intact pawn, in `RimWorld/Pawn_WorkSettings.EnableAndInitialize`'s own order (`AverageOfRelevantSkillsFor` descending). Same three gates as `work-priorities` — the `PawnColumnWorker_WorkPriority.DoCell` early-out, `WorkTypeIsDisabled` (because `SetPriority` answers that with a RED ERROR), and the manual-priorities check. **When nobody remains it says so explicitly** with `gate: \"no-candidate\"` vs `\"too-few-candidates\"` and every count that decides the follow-up: `enabled` short means promote, `capable` short means recruit, an `enabled_but_incapable` list means the fix is surgery. `data.ok` is the verb's own verdict; the envelope stays `ok:true` because a refusal is information (PLAY-LOOP §act).\n\n## Part 2b — `triage`, and the boundary `61794cd` asked to be decided\n\n**DECIDED ONCE: the clock is a property of the PATIENT (`pawn.health.ticks_until_bleedout`, `61794cd`); the rescue estimate is a property of the ROSTER (this verb).** \"How long would they take\" is a pathfind PER CANDIDATE, and putting one in `pawn` or the digest would make the cheapest read in the surface pay for a pathfinder at every glance. Recorded in DESIGN and on `61794cd`. `PawnSerializer.BleedoutBlock` is shared, so the clock a pawn read shows and the clock a rescue is judged against are one builder.\n\nPer casualty (downed OR needs-tend OR bleeding — the union, because `Alert_ColonistNeedsTend` INVERTS, going OFF when the patient goes DOWN): the clock block, every candidate rescuer gated through the SHIPPED `TakeToBedGate(\"rescue\", …)` with the game's own refusal reason, `travel_ticks` and `carry_ticks` for the nearest few, a `verdict` (`in-time` / `too-slow` / `no-rescuer` / `no-path` / `no-deadline`), `margin_ticks`, and **`act` — the exact `rescue` envelope with both ids filled in.**\n\nThat last field is the point. `rescue` was shipped, forces the job through `TryTakeOrderedJob`, interrupts `LayDown`, and was called ZERO times in 195 ops while the response actually tried was a work-priority flip whose chosen rescuer stayed asleep for ~6,100 ticks.\n\n`travel_ticks` is `PawnPath.TotalCost`, whose unit genuinely IS ticks — `Verse.AI/Pawn_PathFollower.CostToMoveIntoCell` spends `TicksPerMoveCardinal|Diagonal + pathGrid.CalculatedCostAt + edifice.PathWalkCostFor`, one per tick. Published as a FLOOR: it excludes door waits, pawn collisions and the time to abandon the current job — which is exactly what `rescue` removes. Pathfinding is capped at `path_candidates` (default 3, by straight-line rank) and the number pathed vs not is published.\n\n## Part 3 is NOT built\n\n\"Refuse to advance when the bleed clock is shorter than the travel time\" belongs on `advance` and therefore on `722c951`'s machinery, which does not exist yet. `triage` publishes both numbers and the verdict, so the refusal has everything it needs to consume. Do not grow a second refusal path here.\n\n## Owed\n\n- The whole acceptance section. Nothing here has met a bench.\n- `checklists/` colony-start item 7 shrinking to \"confirm the mod's read\", and `[[one-doctor-is-zero-doctors]]` keeping only the WHY — untouched, and it is an acceptance bullet.","files":null}]}